B.aEC=new A.qA(1,"45-year-old with neck pain radiating to the thumb...","45-year-old with neck pain radiating to the thumb. Weak biceps (4/5) and brachioradialis (4/5). Diminished biceps reflex. Normal median SNAP.","C6","Biceps and brachioradialis share C6 innervation across different peripheral nerves. The diminished biceps reflex and normal SNAP confirm a preganglionic (root) lesion at C6.",null)
B.aEE=new A.qA(2,"55-year-old runner with back pain radiating to the dorsal foot...","55-year-old runner with back pain radiating to the dorsal foot. Weak ankle dorsiflexion (3/5), great toe extension (2/5), and hip abduction (4/5). Normal ankle jerk. Normal sural SNAP.","L5","Gluteus medius (hip abduction) involvement distinguishes this from fibular neuropathy. The fibular nerve does not innervate gluteus medius. Normal sural SNAP confirms a preganglionic lesion.",null)
B.aEG=new A.qA(3,"60-year-old diabetic with hand clumsiness...","60-year-old diabetic with hand clumsiness. FDI 3/5, APB 4/5, EIP 4/5. All SNAPs normal. Fibs in FDI, APB, EIP, and C8 paraspinals.","C8 Radiculopathy","Normal SNAPs = preganglionic lesion. The key is that FDI (ulnar), APB (median), and EIP (radial) all share the C8 root but are innervated by three different peripheral nerves. Paraspinal fibrillations confirm root-level pathology.",null)
B.aEF=new A.qA(4,"40-year-old after heavy lifting with shoulder pain...","40-year-old after heavy lifting with shoulder pain. Deltoid 3/5, infraspinatus 4/5, biceps 4/5. Triceps 5/5. Diminished biceps reflex. Normal SNAPs.","C5","Deltoid (axillary nerve), infraspinatus (suprascapular nerve), and biceps (musculocutaneous nerve) all share C5 innervation across three different peripheral nerves. Normal triceps (C7) helps exclude a broader plexopathy.",null)
B.aED=new A.qA(5,"65-year-old with calf pain, weak gastroc...","65-year-old with calf pain, weak gastroc (4/5), absent ankle jerk. H-reflex 35ms right vs 29ms left. Normal sural SNAPs.","S1","Gastrocnemius weakness and absent Achilles reflex are classic S1 findings. The prolonged H-reflex (>6ms side-to-side difference) is a sensitive marker for S1 radiculopathy. Normal sural SNAP confirms a preganglionic lesion.",null)
