a=A.cd(v9,a1,s2,"INDICATED (Levels of involvement)",A.a([A.t(!1,"Typically spared as it is the first median branch",f0,k6,f0,f0,w2,h0,f0,f0,o8,f0),A.t(!0,f0,f0,h2,f0,f0,o7,h0,f0,h4,o8,f0),A.t(!0,f0,f0,h2,f0,f0,w3,w4,f0,h4,i0,f0),A.t(!0,f0,f0,h2,f0,f0,g9,h0,f0,h4,h1,f0),A.t(!1,h7,f0,f0,f0,f0,h5,h6,f0,f0,h1,f0),A.t(!1,h7,f0,f0,f0,f0,h8,h9,f0,f0,i0,f0),A.t(!1,f0,f0,f0,f0,f0,w5,f0,f0,f0,f0,f0)],n),"This case localizes the Median neuropathy to the proximal forearm. The involvement of the Palmar Cutaneous branch (causing numbness in the central palm) is a critical clinical differentiator from Carpal Tunnel Syndrome. EMG helps confirm by showing sparing of the Pronator Teres (innervated proximal to the entrapment) while potentially showing denervation in distal median or AIN muscles if the compression is significant.",a0,"pronator",a2,ae8,ae7,!0,a,A.a(["Pronator Teres Syndrome (PTS) is the second most common median nerve entrapment after CTS.","Crucial Distinction: PTS involves the Palmar Cutaneous branch, leading to sensory loss in the central palm. CTS spares this area because the branch arises proximal to the carpal tunnel.","Electrodiagnosis: The Pronator Teres (PT) is typically SPARED on EMG because the entrapment occurs as the nerve passes between the PT heads or under the FDS arch (distal to the PT branch).","NCS may show conduction slowing across the elbow, though this can be technically difficult to demonstrate compared to CTS."],af3),"Forearm Pain and Palmar Numbness")
