a1=A.cd(w8,a3,s2,"INDICATED (High-level localization)",A.a([A.t(!0,f0,f0,j8,f0,f0,w2,h0,f0,h4,o8,f0),A.t(!0,f0,f0,j8,f0,f0,"Flexor Carpi Radialis (FCR)",h0,f0,h4,o8,f0),A.t(!0,f0,f0,j8,f0,f0,w7,w4,f0,h4,i0,f0),A.t(!0,f0,f0,h2,f0,f0,g9,h0,f0,h4,h1,f0),A.t(!1,f0,f0,f0,f0,f0,h5,h6,f0,f0,h1,f0),A.t(!1,f0,f0,f0,f0,f0,r9,u8,f0,f0,v2,f0)],n),"This rare high-median entrapment occurs at the supracondylar process of the humerus, where the Ligament of Struthers compresses the median nerve. Unlike Pronator Teres Syndrome (which spares the PT branch), this lesion is PROXIMAL to the PT branch, causing weakness in the Pronator Teres, FCR, FPL, and APB. Diagnosis is supported by seeing a supracondylar process on X-ray.",a2,"struthers",a4,ae8,ae7,!0,a1,A.a(["Median neuropathy at the Ligament of Struthers is a high-arm entrapment near the supracondylar process.","Classic differentiator from PTS: The Pronator Teres is weak (in PTS, it is spared).","Classic differentiator from CTS: The Forearm muscles and Palm sensation are involved.","Radiographic hallmark: A bony supracondylar process on lateral/AP X-rays of the distal humerus."],af3),"Forearm Weakness with Mid-Arm Pain")
ae7=A.cc(48,"Inability to straighten fingers and thumb, but no numbness",i1,"Noticed difficulty 3 days ago. He can extend his wrist but it 'pulls' towards the thumb side. He cannot lift his fingers or thumb up from a flat surface. No history of trauma. No neck pain. No sensory symptoms at all.",x1,i2,"Osteoarthritis of the elbow")
ae8=A.ci("No visible atrophy (acute).","Deep tenderness over the proximal lateral forearm (over the supinator muscle).","Right Brachioradialis reflex 2+. Triceps reflex 2+. Left reflexes symmetric.",v3,"STRICTLY NORMAL throughout the radial, median, and ulnar distributions. No snuffbox numbness.","Negative 'OK' sign. Negative Tinel's at the wrist.","Right Finger Extension (EDC) 1/5, Thumb Extension (EPL) 1/5, Thumb Abduction (APL) 2/5. Wrist Extension: 4/5 (Presents with RADIAL deviation due to preserved ECRL/ECRB and weak ECU). Brachioradialis and Supinator strength: 5/5.")
a4=A.a([A.Q(x2,d9),A.Q("High Radial Neuropathy (Saturday Night Palsy)","A high radial lesion would involve the Brachioradialis and Supinator and ALWAYS cause sensory loss in the snuffbox/dorsal hand territory."),A.Q(n4,"Radiculopathy would involve the triceps (root-level) and typically cause neck pain and sensory changes in the middle finger."),A.Q("Lead Poisoning Neuropathy","Classically causes Bilateral wrist drop; this case is focal/unilateral and associated with local forearm pain."),A.Q("Extensor Tendon Rupture","Common in Rheumatoid Arthritis; however, tendon rupture would not explain the radial deviation of the wrist or the neurogenic findings on EMG.")],ae9)
a2=A.af(["constitutional","Negative.","musculoskeletal","Lateral elbow/forearm pain.","neurological","Finger/thumb weakness. NO SENSORY LOSS."],af1,af1)
a3=A.a(["None."],af3)
a3=A.cg(A.a(["Ultrasound Forearm: Compression of the radial nerve at the Arcade of Frohse (proximal border of supinator).","MRI Elbow: Synovitis of the radio-humeral joint potentially compressing the deep radial nerve."],af3),a3)
a5=A.a([A.ad(!1,f5,"Crucial finding ruling out high radial neuropathy",f3,f4,f6),A.ad(!1,i7,f0,e6,i6,i8),A.ad(!1,l1,f0,f1,g1,n7)],q)
a6=A.a([A.am(!0,l0,"Low amplitude CMAP from a PIN-innervated muscle",u7,v1,p1),A.am(!1,j4,f0,g6,f7,j5),A.am(!1,g2,f0,g1,g0,g3)],o)
a5=A.ch(A.a([A.db(!1,j7,f2,f4,g5,f0)],m),a6,a5)
a2=A.cd("Posterior Interosseous Nerve Syndrome",a4,s2,"CRITICAL (NCS is characteristically normal)",A.a([A.t(!1,h7,f0,f0,f0,f0,g9,h0,f0,f0,h1,f0),A.t(!1,h7,f0,f0,f0,f0,h5,h6,f0,f0,h1,f0),A.t(!0,f0,f0,q4,f0,f0,h8,h9,f0,s4,i0,f0),A.t(!0,f0,f0,q4,f0,f0,"Extensor Digitorum Communis (EDC)",x3,f0,h4,i0,f0),A.t(!0,f0,f0,j8,f0,f0,x4,x3,f0,h4,i0,f0),A.t(!1,"Spared (Proximal to PIN)",f0,f0,f0,f0,u9,h9,f0,f0,v2,f0),A.t(!1,"Spared (Innervated before/at entry to arcade)",f0,f0,f0,f0,"Supinator",h9,f0,f0,o8,f0),A.t(!1,f0,f0,f0,f0,f0,n9,h9,f0,f0,"C6-C8",f0)],n),"PIN syndrome is a pure motor branch neuropathy of the radial nerve. The hallmark is 'finger drop' with preservation of the Brachioradialis and Supinator (which branch before the PIN enters the Arcade of Frohse). A characteristic finding is Radial Deviation during wrist extension, because the ECRL (Radio-radial) is spared while the ECU (PIN-radial) is weak. Like AIN, the Radial Sensory SNAP is strictly normal, as the PIN carries no cutaneous sensation.",a3,"pin",a5,ae8,ae7,!0,a2,A.a(["PIN syndrome is the radial equivalent of AIN\u2014a pure motor syndrome with strictly Normal Sensation.","Localization Pearl: Preservation of Brachioradialis and Radial Sensory SNAP localizes the lesion distal to the mid-arm/elbow bifurcation.","The 'Radial Deviation' of the wrist is a pathognomonic sign, caused by imbalance between the spared ECRL and the weak ECU.","The most common site of entrapment is the Arcade of Frohse in the supinator muscle."],af3),"Finger Drop without Numbness")
ae7=A.cc(29,"Complete' wrist drop' and numbness on the back of the hand after a long night of sleep",i1,"Woke up after a heavy night of drinking with his arm draped over a hard chair. He found he could not lift his wrist or fingers at all. Significant numbness on the back of the thumb and hand. No neck pain. No trauma.",i4,"Student",i4)
ae8=A.ci("Wrist and fingers hanging in a flexed position (Wrist Drop). No atrophy (hyperacute).","No significant tenderness in the neck or forearm.","Right Brachioradialis reflex 0. Triceps reflex 2+. Left reflexes symmetric.","Full passive ROM. Active extension zero.","Decreased sensation over the radial sensory territory (Snuffbox and dorsal first webspace).","Positive Tinel's sign at the spiral groove of the humerus; Negative Tinel's at the wrist/elbow.","Right Wrist Extension 2/5 (radial deviation only, ECRL partially spared), Finger Extension (EDC) 0/5, Thumb Extension (EPL) 0/5. Brachioradialis 1/5. Supinator 2/5. Triceps strength 5/5. Normal hand intrinsics (APB, ADM, FDI).")
a5=A.a([A.Q("Radial Neuropathy at the Spiral Groove (Saturday Night Palsy)",d9),A.Q(x2,"PIN syndrome spares the Brachioradialis and Supinator and has STRICTLY NORMAL sensation. This case has both BR weakness and snuffbox numbness."),A.Q(n4,"Radiculopathy would typically cause neck pain and involve the triceps; sensory loss would be in the middle finger rather than the first webspace."),A.Q("Brachial Plexopathy (Posterior Cord)","A posterior cord lesion would involve the axillary nerve as well, leading to weak shoulder abduction (Deltoid), which is spared here."),A.Q("Radial Neuropathy at the Axilla","An axillary-level radial lesion (e.g. 'Crutch Palsy') would involve the Triceps, which is spared in this mid-humeral spiral groove injury.")],ae9)
a3=A.af(["constitutional","Negative.","musculoskeletal","No joint pain.","neurological","Wrist drop and dorsal hand numbness."],af1,af1)
a4=A.a(["None."],af3)
a4=A.cg(A.a(["Ultrasound Humerus: Focal swelling of the radial nerve within the spiral groove.","MRI Arm: Normal; no structural mass or fracture."],af3),a4)
a6=A.a([A.ad(!1,"18.0",f0,f3,g1,f9),A.ad(!1,i7,f0,e6,i6,i8),A.ad(!1,l1,f0,f1,g1,n7)],q)
a7=A.a([A.am(!1,i9,f0,e7,"Radial Motor (EIP) - Below Groove",p1),A.am(!0,"0.5","Profound conduction block and slowing across the spiral groove",w1,"Radial Motor (EIP) - Above Groove",f5),A.am(!1,j4,f0,g6,f7,j5),A.am(!1,g2,f0,g1,g0,g3)],o)
a6=A.ch(A.a([A.db(!1,j7,f4,g7,g5,f0)],m),a7,a6)
a3=A.cd("Saturday Night Palsy",a5,k0,"INDICATED (Conduction block vs Axonal loss)",A.a([A.t(!1,h7,f0,f0,f0,f0,g9,h0,f0,f0,h1,f0),A.t(!1,h7,f0,f0,f0,f0,h5,h6,f0,f0,h1,f0),A.t(!0,f0,f0,k6,f0,f0,h8,h9,f0,s4,i0,f0),A.t(!1,"Spared (Proximal to spiral groove)",f0,f0,f0,f0,n9,h9,f0,f0,"C6-C8",f0),A.t(!0,"Neurapraxic block",f0,"0 (Hyperacute)",f0,f0,u9,h9,f0,s4,v2,f0),A.t(!1,f0,f0,k6,f0,s1,"Extensor Carpi Radialis Longus",h9,f0,s1,o8,f0),A.t(!0,f0,f0,k6,f0,f0,"Extensor Digitorum Communis",x3,f0,s4,i0,f0)],n),"This is a classic 'Saturday Night Palsy' localizing to the Spiral Groove of the humerus. The presence of wrist drop, weakness of the Brachioradialis, and sensory loss in the snuffbox confirms a high radial lesion. The sparing of the Triceps (which branches proximal to the spiral groove) narrows the localization to the humerus. In the hyperacute phase, this is often a pure neurapraxia (conduction block).",a4,"saturday_night",a6,ae8,ae7,!0,a3,A.a(["Saturday Night Palsy is a compression neuropathy of the radial nerve at the spiral groove of the humerus.","Key distinction from PIN: High radial lesions involve the Brachioradialis and the Radial Sensory territory (Snuffbox).","Localization Pearl: Sparing of the Triceps points to a lesion after the axilla, typically at the mid-humerus.","Initial NCS often shows dramatic conduction block with preserved distal CMAP amplitudes (until wallerian degeneration occurs)."],af3),"Wrist Drop and Thumb Numbness")
ae7=A.cc(42,"Sudden onset of right knee 'buckling' and numbness in the front of the thigh",i1,"Symptoms started after a long abdominal surgery (inguinal hernia repair). Noticed difficulty going down stairs as the knee gives way. Numbness on the anterior thigh and medial lower leg.",x5,"Sales","Obesity, Type 2 Diabetes")
ae8=A.ci("Mild atrophy of the right quadriceps muscle.","No focal tenderness.","Right Patellar reflex 0 or 1+. Left Patellar reflex 2+. Achilles reflexes 2+ bilaterally.",x6,"Decreased sensation over anterior thigh and medial leg (Saphenous distribution).","Negative straight leg raise.","Right Knee Extension (Quad) 3/5. Hip Flexion (Psoas) 4/5. **Hip Adduction (Adductors) 5/5**.")
a6=A.a([A.Q(x7,d9),A.Q("L3-L4 Radiculopathy","A radiculopathy would involve the hip adductors (Obturator nerve, L2-L4) and the lumbar paraspinal muscles; here adductor strength and paraspinal EMG are normal."),A.Q("Lumbosacral Plexopathy (Upper Trunk)","Plexopathy would typically involve the obturator and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves in addition to the femoral, leading to adductor weakness and wider sensory loss."),A.Q(x8,"Involves only the Lateral Femoral Cutaneous nerve; causes sensory loss on the lateral thigh but NO muscle weakness or reflex changes."),A.Q("Diabetic Amyotrophy","Usually presents with severe pain followed by multi-level proximal weakness (often bilateral or spreading); it is a plexo-radiculopathy and would not be as focal as this post-surgical presentation.")],ae9)
a4=A.af(["constitutional",u6,"musculoskeletal","Right knee 'buckling' and thigh pain. No lower back pain.","neurological","Numbness on the front of the right thigh and the inner side of the lower leg. Difficulty climbing and descending stairs.","endocrine","Type 2 Diabetes (on Metformin)."],af1,af1)
a5=A.a(["HbA1c: 7.2% (Mildly elevated)",p9],af3)
a5=A.cg(A.a(["MRI Pelvis: Small hematoma noted in the right iliacus muscle near the inguinal ligament, consistent with mild compression of the femoral nerve post-surgery.","MRI Lumbar Spine: No significant canal or foraminal stenosis at the L2-L4 levels."],af3),a5)
a7=A.a([A.ad(!1,q1,f0,k5,"3.4","46"),A.ad(!1,q1,f0,k7,p4,q2),A.ad(!0,k6,f0,t2,s4,k6)],q)
a7=A.ch(f0,A.a([A.am(!1,x9,f0,f8,k9,q2),A.am(!1,j4,f0,e7,l2,i7),A.am(!0,y0,f0,"7.8",t3,p1)],o),a7)
a4=A.cd(x7,a6,k0,e2,A.a([A.t(!1,h7,f0,f0,f0,f0,g9,h0,f0,f0,h1,f0),A.t(!1,h7,f0,f0,f0,f0,h5,h6,f0,f0,h1,f0),A.t(!1,h7,f0,f0,f0,f0,h8,h9,f0,f0,i0,f0),A.t(!0,f0,f0,j8,f0,l9,m7,m8,f0,h4,m9,f0),A.t(!1,f0,f0,f0,f0,f0,t7,t8,f0,f0,m9,f0),A.t(!0,f0,f0,h2,f0,f0,n0,n1,f0,h4,n2,f0),A.t(!1,h7,f0,f0,f0,f0,l5,l6,f0,f0,l7,f0),A.t(!1,f0,f0,k6,f0,f0,y1,f0,f0,f0,f0,f0)],n),"Focal femoral neuropathy localizing distal to the plexus. The preservation of hip adduction (Obturator nerve) and normal lumbar paraspinals, combined with weakness in knee extension and an absent patellar reflex, confirms the lesion is isolated to the femoral nerve\u2014likely due to compression or stretch during the recent abdominal surgery.",a5,"femoral",a7,ae8,ae7,!0,a4,A.a(["Femoral neuropathy is often distinguished from L3-L4 radiculopathy by testing the Hip Adductors (Obturator nerve, same roots).","An abnormal Saphenous SNAP localizes the lesion as post-ganglionic (at the nerve or plexus level), whereas a radiculopathy would typically spare the SNAP.","Iatrogenic femoral nerve injury can occur during pelvic or inguinal surgeries due to compression or retractors."],af3),"Buckling Knee and Thigh Numbness")
ae7=A.cc(35,"Severe right shoulder pain for 1 week, followed by weakness in the arm",i1,"Developed deep, agonizing pain in the right shoulder and upper arm that kept him awake at night. Pain subsided after 10 days, but he noticed he couldn't lift his arm or flex his elbow properly. No recent trauma. Had a viral illness 3 weeks ago.",x1,"Painter",i4)
ae8=A.ci("Rapid onset of atrophy in the right deltoid and supraspinatus muscles.","Mild residual tenderness in the axilla.","Right Biceps reflex 1+. Right Triceps reflex 2+. Left reflexes 2+ throughout.","Limited active abduction and elbow flexion due to weakness.","Patchy numbness over the lateral shoulder (axillary nerve patch).","Negative Spurling's maneuver.","Right Shoulder Abduction (Deltoid) 2/5, External Rotation (Infraspinatus) 2/5, Elbow Flexion (Biceps) 3/5. Hand strength 5/5.")
a7=A.a([A.Q(u.i,d9),A.Q("C5-C6 Radiculopathy","Radiculopathy would involve the paraspinal muscles and usually cause neck pain. Patchy involvement of the axillary, suprascapular, and musculocutaneous nerves without paraspinal denervation points to a post-ganglionic lesion."),A.Q(y2,"A trunk lesion would involve all muscles in that division; PTS is characterized by 'patchy' involvement of individual nerves often from different trunks/cords."),A.Q(u3,"While causing pain and limited ROM, a tear would not explain the multi-focal weakness, muscle atrophy, or the neurogenic findings on EMG."),A.Q("Mononeuritis Multiplex","Usually associated with vasculitis and more systemic symptoms; PTS is more likely given the preceding viral illness and focal shoulder girdle predilection.")],ae9)
a5=A.af(["constitutional","Fever and malaise 3 weeks ago (viral prodrome). No weight loss.","musculoskeletal","Deep, agonizing right shoulder pain, now mostly resolved. Weakness in lifting the arm.","neurological","Weakness and muscle wasting in the shoulder. Numbness over the lateral deltoid.","skin","No rashes noted (rules out Herpes Zoster)."],af1,af1)
a6=A.a(["Sedimentation Rate (ESR): 25 mm/hr (Mildly Elevated)","CRP: 1.2 mg/dL (Normal)","HIV/Hepatitis Panel: Negative"],af3)
a6=A.cg(A.a(["MRI Brachial Plexus: T2/STIR hyperintensity and swelling in the upper trunk and individual nerves (Suprascapular, Axillary), consistent with an inflammatory plexitis.","MRI Cervical Spine: Normal; no root compression."],af3),a6)
a8=A.a([A.ad(!1,i7,f0,e6,i6,i8),A.ad(!1,l1,f0,f1,g1,n7),A.ad(!1,f5,f0,f3,f4,f6),A.ad(!0,j2,f0,n8,p4,f9)],q)
