c3=A.cd("Acute Right MCA Territory Ischemic Stroke",c5,d4,"NOT INDICATED",A.a([],n),"The acute onset of hemiplegia with facial involvement, hyperreflexia (UMN), and a positive Babinski sign localizes the lesion to the central nervous system (brain). Non-contrast CT confirming an MCA territory infarct makes electrodiagnostic testing (NCS/EMG) inappropriate and unnecessary.",c4,"stroke",c6,ae8,ae7,!1,c3,A.a(["Electrodiagnostic studies (NCS/EMG) are tests of the PERIPHERAL nervous system (Root, Plexus, Nerve, NMJ, Muscle).","Central nervous system lesions (Stroke, MS, SCI) present with Upper Motor Neuron (UMN) signs: Hyperreflexia, Spasticity, and Babinski sign.","Ordering an EMG for a stroke patient is a common 'trap' in clinical exams; it is not indicated and would delay acute stabilization/thrombectomy.","Standard NCS/EMG will be ENTIRELY NORMAL in the acute setting of a CNS lesion."],af3),"Sudden Onset Left-Sided Weakness")
ae7=A.cc(55,"2-year history of progressive hand numbness and weakness with visible thumb muscle wasting",d5,"Hand symptoms have worsened significantly over 2 years. Constant numbness now affects the first 3.5 digits. Patient noticed she was dropping items and struggled with buttoning shirts. Visible 'flatness' of the thumb muscle emerged over the last 6 months. Works 40 hours/week in manual assembly.","Metformin, Levothyroxine","Assembly Line Worker","Type 2 Diabetes, Hypothyroidism")
ae8=A.ci("Marked atrophy (hollowing) of the right thenar eminence. No calluses on the right thumb.","Mild tenderness over the carpal tunnel. Normal elbow and neck palpation.","2+ and symmetric throughout upper extremities.",v3,"Absent light touch/pinprick in the first 3.5 digits on the palmar surface. Normal sensation on the dorsal hand.","Highly positive Phalen's test (immediate symptoms). Tinel's positive at the wrist.","Thumb Abduction (APB) 3/5. Thumb Opposition 3+/5. Finger abduction (FDI) 5/5. Wrist extension 5/5.")
c6=A.a([A.Q("Severe Carpal Tunnel Syndrome",d9),A.Q(v8,"A C6 radiculopathy would typically spare the Median SNAP (pre-ganglionic); while the SNAP is absent here due to severe nerve damage, the paraspinals and non-median C6 muscles are normal."),A.Q("Median Neuropathy at the Elbow (Pronator)","Pronator syndrome would involve proximal median-innervated muscles (FCR, PT) and cause sensory loss in the palm (palmar cutaneous branch); here those are spared."),A.Q("Grip/Motor Branch Entrapment","Would cause weakness but not the profound sensory loss seen here."),A.Q(ac1,"Polyneuropathy is typically symmetric and affects the feet first (stocking-glove); here findings are focal to the Median nerve.")],ae9)
